ACC-015 — Engineering Constraints

Document ID: ACC-015
Title: Engineering Constraints
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines Engineering Constraints, the limitations, requirements, and boundaries that govern engineering cognition within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

Engineering Constraints ensure that Engineering Understanding, Engineering Reasoning, Engineering Capabilities, and Decision Support operate within applicable physical, technical, regulatory, organizational, and operational limits.

2. Scope

This specification applies to all engineering cognition performed within Atlas.

Engineering Constraints may include, but are not limited to:

Physical laws
Engineering standards and codes
Regulatory requirements
Safety requirements
Design criteria
Material properties
Resource availability
Budget limitations
Schedule limitations
Environmental conditions
Operational limitations
Manufacturing limitations
Construction limitations
Stakeholder requirements
Organizational policies

Engineering Constraints apply throughout the engineering cognition lifecycle.

3. Canonical Definition

Engineering Constraints are the explicit limitations, requirements, and governing conditions that define the permissible boundaries of engineering cognition and engineering outcomes.

Engineering Constraints define what must be respected, regardless of the engineering objective.

4. Characteristics

Engineering Constraints SHALL possess the following characteristics.

4.1 Explicit

Engineering Constraints SHALL be explicitly identified whenever practical.

4.2 Governing

Engineering Constraints SHALL define permissible engineering boundaries.

4.3 Context-Dependent

The applicability of an Engineering Constraint SHALL depend upon the relevant Engineering Context.

4.4 Traceable

Engineering Constraints SHALL remain traceable throughout engineering cognition.

4.5 Verifiable

Compliance with Engineering Constraints SHALL be capable of verification.

4.6 Evolvable

Engineering Constraints MAY change as regulations, standards, technologies, or project conditions evolve.

4.7 Hierarchical

Engineering Constraints MAY possess different levels of authority and precedence.

For example:

Physical laws
Legal and regulatory requirements
Engineering standards
Organizational policies
Project-specific requirements

Conflicts SHOULD be resolved according to their governing authority.

